From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] scrrun, wshom: text streams follow Line and Column, pipes and standard streams read, Exec's output arrives

A program's output read through WshShell.Exec(...).StdOut -- how scripts
capture a command's output -- was always empty: Exec started the program
without letting it inherit the pipes, and a pipe stream could only be
written (ReadLine, ReadAll, Read, AtEndOfStream and the rest were
E_NOTIMPL). Scripting.FileSystemObject's text streams lacked Line, Column,
AtEndOfLine, WriteBlankLines, Skip and SkipLine, and GetStandardStream was
E_NOTIMPL. A failed read released the stream once too often.

- WshShell.Exec: the program inherits its standard handles (the pipes).
- Pipe streams (Exec's StdIn/StdOut/StdErr, DoOpenPipeStream) read as
  file streams do; a pipe ends when its writer goes, and AtEndOfStream
  waits for data or that end.
- Line and Column follow what is read and written (1-based; a line feed
  starts a new line).
- AtEndOfLine (at a line's end or the stream's; on a stream opened for
  writing, CTL_E_BADFILEMODE and False), WriteBlankLines, Skip, SkipLine.
- GetStandardStream gives the process's standard input, output or error
  as a stream of its own (Unicode or not).
- Win32 errors scripts meet (sharing violations, a full disk, bad names,
  drives not ready, directories not empty) map to their Visual Basic
  errors; others are the Win32 error's HRESULT rather than E_FAIL.

Wine's scrrun filesystem tests: six todo_wine pass and are removed; wshom
tests: no change.

Gate: test/textstreams-gate.sh (64- and 32-bit cscript with
test/textstreams.vbs and input on its stdin). Mutants
SG_MUTANT_PIPE_EOF_EARLY, SG_MUTANT_NO_POSITION (scrrun/filesystem.c).
